# module|page|topic|question|correct|wrong 1|wrong 2|wrong 3|explanation
1|2|Early history|How did many early Filipino builders learn construction skills before Spanish colonization?|Through a master–apprentice system in the community|Through a national licensure examination|Through standardized government blueprints|Through a university architecture degree|The module describes practical skills passed from masters to apprentices within communities.
1|2|Early history|What title was used for a master builder during the Spanish period?|Maestro de Obras|Architect of Record|Construction Manager|Professional Consulting Architect|Maestro de Obras was the historical title for a master builder.
1|2|Early history|Which school offered training for Maestro de Obras in Manila in 1890?|Escuela Practica y Profesional de Artes y Oficios de Manila|University of the Philippines School of Architecture|Mapúa School of Architecture|Philippine Institute of Architects|The module identifies this Manila school as the formal training institution established in 1890.
1|2|Early history|Which distinction was made in the historical training of Maestro de Obras?|Académico and Práctico|Designer and contractor|Private and public|Local and foreign|The module distinguishes academic and practical paths or titles in the early training system.
1|2|Early history|Who is described in the module as the first Filipino architect?|Félix Roxas y Arroyo|Juan Nakpil|Tomás Mapúa|Arcadio Arellano|The module names Félix Roxas y Arroyo as the first Filipino architect.
1|2|Early history|Where did Félix Roxas y Arroyo receive architectural training?|London|Paris|Rome|Tokyo|The historical account states that he trained in London.
1|2|Early history|What public position did Félix Roxas y Arroyo hold from 1877 to 1880?|Manila Municipal Architect|PRC Board Chair|UAP President|Secretary of Public Works|He served as Manila Municipal Architect during those years.
1|2|Early history|Which building is associated with Félix Roxas y Arroyo in the module?|Ayuntamiento|Crystal Arcade|Veterans Memorial Hospital|Manila City Hall|The module lists the Ayuntamiento among his works.
1|2|Early history|Who was among the earliest Filipino graduates of the Maestro de Obras course in 1891?|Julio Hernández|Carlos Barreto|Andrés Luna de San Pedro|José Herrera|Julio Hernández is listed as an 1891 graduate.
1|2|Early history|Which future architect graduated from the Maestro de Obras course in 1894?|Arcadio Arellano|Tomás Mapúa|Juan Nakpil|Guillermo Gardiner|Arcadio Arellano is among the 1894 graduates named by the module.
1|2|Organizations|What was the first organization of architects and surveyors in the Philippines according to the module?|Academia de Arquitectura y Agremensura de Filipinas|United Architects of the Philippines|Philippine Institute of Architects|League of Philippine Architects|The module identifies this 1902 academy as the first organization of architects and surveyors.
1|2|Organizations|Who led the architects and surveyors association founded in 1902?|Guillermo Gardiner|Juan Nakpil|Tomás Mapúa|Carlos da Silva|Guillermo Gardiner is named as its leader.
1|3|Organizations|What was the primary significance of the 1903 Tarifa de Honorarios?|It established early professional practice and fee standards|It created the PRC|It introduced the National Building Code|It founded the UAP|The fee tariff supplied the first practice standards and fee schedules discussed in the module.
1|3|Organizations|What did the 1902 academy become in 1903?|Academia de Arquitectura, Ingeniería y Agremensura de Filipinas|Philippine Institute of Architects|United Architects of the Philippines|Professional Regulation Commission|The group expanded to include engineers and took the AIAAF name.
1|3|Licensing history|What did Philippine Assembly Act No. 2985 of 1921 establish?|Separate examiner boards for architecture and civil engineering|The current Architecture Act|The UAP as IAPOA|The National Building Code|The 1921 act separated the two professions' examination boards.
1|3|Licensing history|Who received architect license number 1 in the historical account?|Tomás Mapúa|Juan Nakpil|Félix Roxas y Arroyo|Antonio Toledo|The module lists Tomás Mapúa as architect license number 1.
1|3|Licensing history|Who received architect license number 2 in the historical account?|Carlos Barreto|Tomás Mapúa|Antonio Toledo|Juan Nakpil|Carlos Barreto is listed as license number 2.
1|3|Licensing history|Who received architect license number 3 in the historical account?|Antonio Toledo|Carlos Barreto|Juan Nakpil|Arcadio Arellano|Antonio Toledo is listed as license number 3.
1|3|Organizations|Which organization was formed in 1933 with Juan Nakpil as president?|Philippine Architects' Society|United Architects of the Philippines|Architects National Code Committee|PRC Board of Architecture|The 1933 Philippine Architects' Society named Juan Nakpil as president.
1|3|Organizations|Who served as vice president of the 1933 Philippine Architects' Society?|Tomás Mapúa|Félix Roxas y Arroyo|José Herrera|Carlos da Silva|Tomás Mapúa is listed as vice president.
1|3|Licensing history|What separation did the proposed 1938 Bill No. 1850 seek?|Further separation of architecture from civil engineering|Separation of schools from universities|Separation of contractors from suppliers|Separation of public and private buildings|The proposal concerned the professions of architecture and civil engineering.
1|3|Organizations|Which later organization emerged from the Philippine Architects' Society after World War II?|Philippine Institute of Architects|United Architects Inc.|League of Philippine Architects|Architects' National Code|The module traces PAS through PIAP to the Philippine Institute of Architects.
1|3|Organizations|Which splinter organization was headed by José Herrera?|League of Philippine Architects|Philippine Institute of Architects|Architects and Surveyors Association|Professional Regulation Commission|José Herrera is named in connection with the League of Philippine Architects.
1|3|Organizations|Which separate group pursued the Veterans Memorial Hospital project?|United Architects Inc.|League of Philippine Architects|Philippine Architects' Society|UAP|The historical narrative associates United Architects Inc. with that project.
1|4|Licensing history|Which 1948 law allowed private architects and engineers to undertake public projects?|Republic Act No. 472|Republic Act No. 545|Republic Act No. 9266|Presidential Decree No. 223|The module attributes this change to RA 472.
1|4|Licensing history|Which 1949 law regulated architecture before RA 9266 replaced it?|Republic Act No. 545|Republic Act No. 544|Republic Act No. 472|Republic Act No. 1581|RA 545 was the earlier architecture law that RA 9266 repealed.
1|4|Licensing history|Which 1949 law concerned civil engineering?|Republic Act No. 544|Republic Act No. 545|Republic Act No. 9266|Philippine Assembly Act No. 2985|The paired 1949 laws are RA 544 for civil engineering and RA 545 for architecture.
1|4|Recognition|Which architect received a gold medal associated with Crystal Arcade in 1950?|Andrés Luna de San Pedro|Juan Nakpil|Tomás Mapúa|Carlos da Silva|The module links Andrés Luna de San Pedro, the 1950 honor, and Crystal Arcade.
1|4|Recognition|Who became an honorary AIA member in 1954 according to the module?|Carlos da Silva|Juan Nakpil|Guillermo Gardiner|José Herrera|Carlos da Silva is the architect named in the 1954 entry.
1|4|Organizations|Which organization became the UIA national section in 1957?|Philippine Institute of Architects|United Architects Inc.|League of Philippine Architects|Architects National Code Committee|The module dates PIA's UIA national-section role to 1957.
1|4|Organizations|What was APGA in the module's unification history?|An architects' organization formed in 1958|The 1921 licensing board|A construction contractor association|A university architecture school|APGA is one of the architect organizations involved in later unification.
1|4|Organizations|What did APGA, LPA, and PIA approve together in 1965?|An Architects National Code covering services and fees|The first Architecture Act|The National Building Code|A single licensure examination timetable|The module describes their joint approval of a national code for services and fees.
1|4|Organizations|Which three organizations were central to 1973 unification talks?|PIA, LPA, and APGA|PRC, CHED, and DPWH|UAP, PICE, and SEC|PAS, AIA, and UIA|The three pre-unification architect organizations were PIA, LPA, and APGA.
1|4|Organizations|What government body did Presidential Decree No. 223 create?|Professional Regulation Commission|Commission on Higher Education|Securities and Exchange Commission|Department of Public Works and Highways|PD 223 created the PRC.
1|5|Organizations|What did the architects choose the name United Architects of the Philippines to signal?|Unity of the profession|A merger with engineers|A government monopoly|A foreign affiliation|The name reflected the unification effort described in the module.
1|5|Organizations|When was the UAP registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission?|March 26, 1975|February 23, 1921|April 10, 2004|January 1, 1965|The module gives March 26, 1975 as the SEC registration date.
1|5|Organizations|Which designation did PRC Certificate No. 001 give the UAP in May 1975?|Integrated and Accredited Professional Organization of Architects|Professional Regulatory Board of Architecture|National Building Official|International Union of Architects|The certificate recognized the UAP as the IAPOA.
1|5|Organizations|Did all members of the Philippine Institute of Architects join the UAP immediately?|No, some remained in a separate PIA|Yes, all joined on the same day|No, none joined|The module does not mention PIA|The module notes that some PIA members continued separately.
1|5|Organizations|Which global body recognized the UAP as Philippine national section in 1988?|International Union of Architects|World Health Organization|UNESCO|American Institute of Architects|The module identifies the UIA as the international architects' body.
1|5|Licensing history|Which law currently governs architectural practice in the Philippines in the module?|Republic Act No. 9266|Republic Act No. 545|Republic Act No. 544|Presidential Decree No. 1096|RA 9266, the Architecture Act of 2004, replaced RA 545.
1|5|Licensing history|When did RA 9266 take effect according to the module?|April 10, 2004|March 26, 1975|February 23, 1921|December 1, 2004|The module states that the act took effect on April 10, 2004.
1|5|Licensing history|What type of documents are central to the signing-and-sealing dispute discussed in the module?|Architectural documents|Payroll documents|Land titles only|School transcripts|The dispute concerns who may prepare and sign architectural documents.
1|6|Licensing history|What is Presidential Decree No. 1096 commonly called?|The National Building Code of the Philippines|The Architecture Act of 2004|The Labor Code|The UAP Constitution|PD 1096 is the National Building Code.
1|6|Licensing history|What was the focus of the disputed Section 302 implementing rules discussed in the historical timeline?|Who could sign architectural permit documents|The number of architecture schools|UAP membership fees|The length of apprenticeship|The historical dispute concerned architectural document signatures in permit rules.
1|7|Professional role|Which set best expresses the architect's responsibility to the built environment?|Safety, health, function, and beauty|Profit, speed, branding, and advertising|Drawing, printing, copying, and filing|Marketing, procurement, litigation, and sales|The module frames architecture around human needs and a safe, functional, beautiful environment.
1|7|Professional role|Which virtue did Vitruvius associate with an architect?|High-mindedness|Avarice|Arrogance|Self-promotion|The module includes high-mindedness among Vitruvius's virtues.
1|7|Professional role|What was Vitruvius's view of gifts that could compromise an architect?|The architect should avoid them|They should be accepted from every contractor|They are mandatory in competitions|They replace professional fees|The module's virtue list rejects gifts and avarice.
1|7|Professional role|Which duty extends beyond simply following a client's requested design?|Protecting the public's health, safety, and welfare|Maximizing the client's publicity|Selecting the cheapest material regardless of safety|Avoiding consultation with other professionals|The architect's broader responsibility includes the public interest.
1|7|Services|Which service category occurs before regular design and can include feasibility work?|Pre-design services|Post-construction services|Construction management|Design-build by administration|Feasibility and early project studies belong to pre-design services.
1|7|Services|Which service category includes the architect's core design process?|Regular design services|Post-construction services|Building administration|Property management|The module lists design as a major architectural service distinct from other engagements.
1|7|Services|Which category covers technical expertise such as acoustics and lighting?|Specialized allied services|Post-construction services|Contractor labor|Property leasing|Specialized allied services supplement the design with specific expertise.
1|7|Services|Which category applies to services during actual building work?|Construction services|Pre-design services|Post-occupancy evaluation|Feasibility studies|Construction services relate to the implementation period.
1|7|Services|Which category applies after a building is complete and occupied?|Post-construction services|Pre-design services|Schematic design|Bidding services|Post-construction services support operation and evaluation after completion.
1|7|Services|Which service category coordinates several architectural service stages into one engagement?|Comprehensive architectural services|Site selection alone|Drafting only|Engineering design alone|Comprehensive services cover multiple stages of professional work.
1|7|Services|Which service model includes both design and construction responsibility?|Design-build services|Pre-design consultation|Site selection|Post-occupancy evaluation|Design-build joins design with responsibility for construction delivery.
1|8|Services|Can the seven major architectural service categories be contracted separately?|Yes, each can be a separate professional engagement|No, all seven must always be in one contract|Only design-build can be separate|Only post-construction can be separate|The module notes that the service categories may be engaged separately.
1|8|Services|Which document helps define a client's functional requirements before design proceeds?|Architectural program|Occupancy permit|Final payment certificate|As-built drawing|The program organizes needs and requirements for design.
1|8|Services|Which document records construction details for a contractor to build from?|Construction documents|Feasibility sketch only|Publicity brochure|Tenant billing schedule|Construction documents convey the approved design and technical information for building.
1|8|Services|Which post-construction output supports the owner's operation of a building?|Operations and maintenance manual|Initial site shortlist|Design competition brief|Preliminary cost estimate|Operating and maintenance guidance belongs to post-construction work.
1|8|Services|At what broad point can an architect's professional work begin?|At the first project idea|Only after occupancy|Only when excavation begins|Only after a contractor is hired|The module presents the architect's service spectrum from project conception onward.
1|8|Services|At what broad point can an architect's professional work continue?|Into operation and use of the building|Only until concept approval|Only until permit filing|Only until the first tender|The service spectrum extends through use and post-construction support.
2|2|Education|Which agency grants authority to private institutions to operate BS Architecture programs?|Commission on Higher Education|Professional Regulation Commission|Department of Public Works and Highways|Securities and Exchange Commission|The module identifies CHED as the higher-education regulator for private BS Architecture programs.
2|2|Education|What does PSG stand for in architecture education?|Policies, Standards, and Guidelines|Professional Services Group|Project Specifications Guide|Practice and Supervision Guarantee|PSG is the education framework named in the module.
2|2|Education|What educational approach do the Architecture Education PSG promote?|Outcomes-based education|Memorization without competencies|Construction-only training|Self-certification of degrees|The PSG define competencies and minimum learning outcomes.
2|2|Education|What do Architecture Education PSG specify in addition to outcomes?|Minimum credit units|Contractor profit margins|Building permit fees|Architect fee percentages|The PSG also set minimum credit-unit requirements.
2|2|Licensure|Which law supplies the architecture licensure framework in the module?|Republic Act No. 9266|Republic Act No. 544|Republic Act No. 472|Presidential Decree No. 1096|RA 9266 is the Architecture Act of 2004.
2|3|Licensure|Which professional credential is needed to lawfully practice as an architect?|Registration and a valid professional identification card|A drafting certificate alone|A contractor's license alone|An architecture student ID|The act requires registration and the professional identification card for practice.
2|3|Licensure|How much diversified architectural experience is normally required before the licensure exam?|Two years|Three months|Six years|No experience|The module states a two-year post-baccalaureate pre-licensure experience requirement.
2|3|Licensure|Who must certify a candidate's diversified architectural experience?|A registered and licensed architect|Any construction laborer|A classmate|A building-material supplier|The candidate's architectural experience is certified by a registered and licensed architect.
2|3|Licensure|How much practical experience can a relevant master's degree credit under the module's rule?|One year|Two years|Three months|None|The module states that a master's degree may be credited as one year of practical experience.
2|3|Licensure|What academic degree is ordinarily required to apply for the architect licensure examination?|BS Architecture from a CHED-recognized institution|Any four-year science degree|Civil engineering diploma alone|A short drafting certificate|The eligibility list specifies a recognized architecture bachelor's degree.
2|3|Licensure|Which personal qualification is specifically required of licensure applicants?|Good moral character|Membership in a construction firm|Ownership of land|A foreign license in all cases|Good moral character appears among the statutory qualifications.
2|3|Licensure|Which record may be required as evidence of a candidate's criminal-history status?|NBI clearance|Material delivery receipt|Site grading plan|Property tax declaration|The module lists NBI clearance among supporting documents.
2|4|Licensure|Which exam subject set includes historical and professional knowledge?|History, Theory, Planning, and Practice|Structural steel only|Equipment maintenance only|Real estate leasing only|This is one of the broad licensure subject areas listed in RA 9266.
2|4|Licensure|Which exam subject set includes building systems and construction?|Structures, Materials, Construction, and Utilities|History alone|Interior decoration alone|Property management alone|These technical topics form an exam subject group.
2|4|Licensure|Which exam subject set combines design with the site?|Architectural Design and Site Planning|History and ethics only|Accounting and taxation|Property leasing|The module lists Architectural Design and Site Planning together.
2|4|Licensure|What general weighted average is required to pass the exam under the module?|At least 70 percent|At least 50 percent|Exactly 60 percent|At least 95 percent|RA 9266's passing standard is a weighted general average of at least 70 percent.
2|4|Licensure|What minimum mark must a passing candidate avoid falling below in any subject?|50 percent|70 percent|80 percent|90 percent|The candidate must have no subject grade below 50 percent.
2|6|Diversified training|When does diversified architectural experience ordinarily begin?|After completing the architecture bachelor's degree|Before enrolling in architecture school|Only after licensure|Only after retirement|The module defines it as post-baccalaureate, pre-licensure experience.
2|6|Diversified training|Why is the required experience described as diversified?|It covers various phases of architectural service|It occurs only in different cities|It requires multiple universities|It focuses only on drafting|The trainee must experience multiple phases of architectural work.
2|6|Diversified training|Which document records the trainee's architectural experience by field of practice?|DT Form 001|Occupancy permit|Building tax declaration|Material purchase order|The module assigns the field-of-practice summary to DT Form 001.
2|6|Diversified training|Which document is completed by the mentor for project experience?|DT Form 002|Building permit form|Land title|PRC ID application only|The mentor documents project periods and hours on DT Form 002.
2|6|Diversified training|What total training time does the module require on DT Form 001?|At least 3,840 hours|At least 500 hours|Exactly 1,000 hours|At least 10,000 hours|The logbook guidance totals two years as at least 3,840 hours.
2|7|Diversified training|How many work hours does the module use for a full-time training day?|Eight|Four|Ten|Twelve|The module calculates full-time days using eight hours per day.
2|7|Diversified training|How should extra hours in one field of practice affect a shortfall in another?|They should not be transferred across fields|They automatically cover every shortfall|They double the overall total|They become examination points|The logbook directions prohibit moving excess hours between FOP categories.
2|7|Diversified training|Which mark is entered after the final experience entry on a mentor form?|Nothing Follows|Approved for construction|As-built|Final Occupancy|The module instructs mentors to write “Nothing Follows” after the last project entry.
2|7|Diversified training|What should the mentor apply to the completed DT Form 002?|Signature and dry seal|Only a verbal approval|A contractor's stamp|A school registrar's stamp|The form instructions require the mentor's signature and dry seal.
2|11|Practice law|What does the professional identification card establish alongside registration?|Current authority to practice under the Act|Ownership of an architecture firm|A building's occupancy status|Ownership of all project drawings|Section 25 requires registration and a professional identification card to practice.
2|12|Practice law|What is the purpose of the Act's reciprocity provision?|It sets conditions for foreign architects to practice|It waives all licensure requirements for anyone|It replaces local building permits|It sets architect fee percentages|Reciprocity concerns equivalent practice rights for qualified foreign nationals.
2|12|Practice law|What does CPD refer to in the professional practice rules?|Continuing Professional Development|Construction Price Discount|Certified Project Drawing|Contractor Payment Date|CPD concerns ongoing professional learning.
2|13|Practice law|Which act is prohibited under the architecture law?|Impersonating a registered architect|Working with a structural engineer|Explaining a design to a client|Conducting a site analysis|The prohibited-acts section includes impersonation and related fraud.
2|13|Practice law|What should formalize an architect's scope and compensation with a client?|A written service contract|An unsigned verbal rumor|A supplier's purchase order|A student's logbook|The module identifies a written contract as the required professional agreement.
2|14|Practice law|Who should sign and seal architectural documents prepared under an architect's responsible direction?|The architect of record|Any contractor|The materials supplier|Any unlicensed graduate|The architect of record signs and seals work within their professional responsibility.
2|14|Practice law|Can a registered architect simply sign someone else's architectural drawings without responsible involvement?|No, the architect must take responsibility for their own work|Yes, a signature has no liability|Yes, if the sheets are already printed|Only when a supplier requests it|The act connects signing and sealing to professional responsibility and liability.
2|14|Practice law|Can an owner freely reuse an architect's signed plans for another project without written consent?|No, written consent is needed|Yes, the plans become public domain|Yes, if the new site is nearby|Only when no contractor is present|The module treats signed architectural documents as the architect's intellectual property.
2|15|Practice law|Can an unregistered person claim to provide equivalent architectural services under another label?|No, that would evade the practice restrictions|Yes, by avoiding the word architect|Yes, if no fee is charged|Yes, if the client agrees verbally|The module prohibits unregistered persons from holding out equivalent architectural services.
2|15|Practice law|Who may properly collect a fee for architectural services under the module's practice rules?|A registered architect or authorized representative|Any unregistered broker|A materials supplier|A student assistant acting alone|The module reserves collection for the architect, with an employee or representative exception.
2|16|Practice law|What minimum architect ownership share does the module state for an architectural firm?|75 percent|25 percent|50 percent|100 percent in every case|The firm rules in the supplied module specify at least 75 percent registered-architect ownership.
2|16|Practice law|What additional requirement applies to firms offering architectural services?|Registration with the SEC and the professional board|Only a social-media page|Only a contractor license|Only a supplier agreement|The module describes firm registration requirements beyond individual licensure.
2|17|Practice law|What does a foreign architect generally need for a Philippine project under the module?|A special permit and Filipino architect counterpart|Only a tourist visa|Only an overseas diploma|Only client permission|Section 38 describes special permits and a Filipino counterpart architect of record.
2|17|Practice law|What is one reason the module requires a Filipino counterpart for a foreign architect?|Local professional responsibility and technology transfer|To replace all engineers|To avoid written contracts|To remove building-code review|The local counterpart and transfer of expertise are part of the special-permit framework.
2|18|Practice law|What risk does malpractice or professional-liability insurance address?|Claims arising from professional errors or omissions|Construction material price changes only|School tuition increases|UAP election costs|Section 39 addresses professional liability coverage.
2|19|Ethics|What should an architect prioritize when a client's instruction would endanger occupants?|Public health, safety, and welfare|The client's desired deadline regardless of risk|The cheapest product available|The contractor's convenience|The code of ethical conduct places legal duties and public safety first.
2|19|Ethics|How should an architect describe a claimed specialty to the public?|Accurately and without unsupported claims|With any title that attracts clients|As a guaranteed outcome|Only through anonymous advertising|The ethics code discourages misleading advertising and unverified specialties.
2|20|Ethics|What should an architect clarify with a client at the start of an engagement?|Scope, responsibilities, and fees|Only the color palette|Only the contractor's brand|Only the final photograph|Clear service terms help define obligations and avoid disputes.
2|20|Ethics|What should an architect do if a client's proposed project is impracticable?|Warn the client and explain the concern|Promise it will work regardless|Conceal the problem until tender|Transfer blame to the contractor immediately|The ethical duties include candid advice about an impracticable proposal.
2|20|Ethics|How should an architect handle a conflict of interest?|Disclose it to the client|Hide it if the fee is small|Wait until after final payment|Ask the supplier to decide|The code requires transparency about interests that can affect professional judgment.
2|20|Ethics|What is the proper treatment of a contractor's secret kickback offered to the architect?|Reject it|Accept it as part of normal fees|Credit it only to the contractor|Conceal it from the owner|The ethics code bars improper compensation that compromises independent judgment.
2|20|Ethics|What dispute-resolution provision should appear in the service agreement?|An alternative dispute resolution clause|A clause forbidding all communication|A clause waiving public safety|A clause transferring authorship to suppliers|The module calls for an ADR provision in the owner-architect agreement.
2|21|Ethics|How should an architect assess construction materials that do not meet approved specifications?|Reject the nonconforming materials|Approve them to maintain progress|Ignore them if inexpensive|Ask the supplier to self-certify without review|Ethical construction duties include rejecting nonconforming work and materials.
2|21|Ethics|How should a supplier discount that belongs to a project be handled?|Credit it to the client|Keep it secretly as an architect bonus|Give it to an unrelated contractor|Use it to replace professional fees without disclosure|The ethics code directs that supplier discounts be credited to the client.
2|21|Ethics|What should an architect avoid accepting from a supplier?|Gifts that could influence professional judgment|Product information|Technical data sheets|Samples requested for review|The code prohibits gifts or benefits that compromise impartial selection.
2|22|Ethics|What should an architect do before taking over another architect's commission?|Notify the predecessor appropriately|Copy the predecessor's drawings without notice|Claim authorship of completed work|Cancel the client's written agreement unilaterally|The code sets professional courtesy rules when replacing another architect.
2|22|Ethics|How should an architect treat a colleague's work product?|Respect its intellectual property|Sign it as personal work without review|Reproduce it for every client|Sell it without permission|The code requires respect for colleagues' authorship and intellectual property.
2|22|Ethics|What should an architect provide employees and apprentices?|Mentorship and fair professional treatment|Only unpaid work without guidance|Automatic licensure|Authority to seal drawings|The code addresses guidance and fair treatment of junior staff.
2|23|Ethics|Which item belongs in a written agreement between collaborating architects?|Scope and division of responsibilities|Only a project nickname|Only office furniture costs|Only social-media credits|The module lists scope, responsibilities, fees, ADR, and termination among agreement terms.
2|23|Ethics|Which ethical rule applies to professional criticism of another architect?|Avoid unfair or malicious criticism|Publish unsupported accusations|Hide factual technical concerns|Never speak to colleagues|The code calls for fair treatment of peers and avoids unjust attacks.
2|24|Ethics|What broad commitment appears in the Architect's Credo?|Service to people and the profession with integrity|Priority to personal fame over safety|Avoidance of civic responsibility|Replacing all allied professionals|The Credo presents architecture as a profession of service and ethical responsibility.
3|2|Framework|What does SPP stand for in the supplied Module 3?|Standards of Professional Practice|Site Planning Permit|Structural Project Program|Supplier Procurement Procedure|The module organizes architectural services through the Standards of Professional Practice.
3|3|Framework|Which body regulates registered architects through the PRC?|Professional Regulatory Board of Architecture|Commission on Higher Education|Securities and Exchange Commission|Department of Tourism|The PRBoA is the architecture regulatory board under PRC.
3|3|Framework|What does RLA mean in the module?|Registered and Licensed Architect|Regional Land Authority|Regular Licensed Associate|Registered Legal Adviser|RLA is the module's abbreviation for a registered and licensed architect.
3|3|Framework|What does AoR mean?|Architect of Record|Assistant of Resources|Architect on Retainer|Authority of Registration|The AoR is the architect responsible for identified professional documents and services.
3|3|Framework|What does IAPOA mean?|Integrated and Accredited Professional Organization of Architects|International Association of Project Owners and Architects|Independent Architectural Planning Office Association|Institute of Allied Professional Organizations|This is the professional organization designation used throughout the SPP.
3|4|Selection|Which architect-selection method suits a small or straightforward commission?|Direct selection|Anonymous design competition only|Random lottery|Construction bidding only|The module associates direct selection with smaller or simpler engagements.
3|4|Selection|What is usually compared in comparative architect selection?|Qualifications and proposed approach|Only office size|Only a contractor's machinery|Only the lowest printing cost|Comparative selection evaluates capability, interviews, verification, and ranking.
3|4|Selection|What should an owner issue to guide invited architects in comparative selection?|Terms of reference|Occupancy certificate|As-built drawings|Final payment certificate|The ToR sets the project scope and selection basis.
3|4|Selection|Which method compares submitted design proposals for an important civic project?|Architectural design competition|Direct appointment of a supplier|Material bidding only|Building administration|The module proposes ADCs for projects where competing design ideas matter.
3|5|Predesign|Which SPP document covers pre-design services?|SPP 201|SPP 202|SPP 205|SPP 208|SPP Document 201 is the pre-design services standard.
3|5|Predesign|What is the main purpose of pre-design services?|Define the project before regular design|Inspect only completed construction|Manage tenant rent collection|Produce only a final occupancy permit|Pre-design work helps clarify project needs, site, viability, and brief.
3|5|Predesign|Which pre-design study relies mainly on readily available secondary information?|Pre-feasibility study|Detailed feasibility study|Construction supervision|Post-occupancy evaluation|The module distinguishes pre-feasibility's secondary data from fuller viability investigation.
3|5|Predesign|Which pre-design study examines project viability using primary investigation?|Feasibility study|Schematic design|Final inspection|Material procurement|Feasibility goes beyond preliminary information to test whether the project is workable.
3|6|Predesign|Which service compares potential locations for a project?|Site selection and analysis|Post-construction evaluation|Contractor billing|Design-build administration|Site selection evaluates alternative locations and their suitability.
3|6|Predesign|What does architectural programming organize?|The owner's activities, needs, and space requirements|A contractor's payroll|Only exterior paint colors|The final certificate of occupancy|Programming turns the client's needs into a usable design brief.
3|6|Predesign|What does space planning primarily arrange?|Functional relationships and use of interior areas|Legal ownership of the land|Reinforcement schedules|Bidding bonds|Space planning distributes functions and circulation within available area.
3|6|Predesign|What does value management seek during project planning?|Better value relative to function and cost|The lowest cost regardless of performance|A larger fee without added work|A substitute for safety standards|Value management tests alternatives against project function and cost.
3|6|Predesign|What document summarizes objectives and requirements for the design team?|Design brief|Final payment certificate|Occupancy permit|Insurance policy|The design brief communicates the agreed project goals and constraints.
3|7|Design phases|Which SPP document governs regular design services?|SPP 202|SPP 201|SPP 204B|SPP 209|SPP Document 202 is the regular design services standard.
3|7|Design phases|Which phase comes first in the module's regular design sequence?|Project definition|Construction|Contract documents|Bidding and negotiation|Project definition establishes the brief before schemes are prepared.
3|7|Design phases|What is the purpose of schematic design?|Develop initial design alternatives and overall concepts|Certify final contractor payment|Operate the completed building|Prepare only tenant bills|Schematic design explores the project's basic arrangement and concept.
3|8|Design phases|What follows schematic design in regular design services?|Design development|Post-occupancy evaluation|Building administration|Final inspection|Design development refines the chosen scheme before final documentation.
3|8|Design phases|What is developed during the design development phase?|More detailed plans, systems, and coordinated design decisions|Only a site shortlist|Only a construction punch list|Only a rental schedule|The phase expands the approved schematic design into coordinated detail.
3|8|Design phases|Which phase produces drawings and specifications for construction and procurement?|Contract documents|Project definition|Post-occupancy evaluation|Site selection|Contract documents communicate the design for permits, pricing, and construction.
3|9|Design phases|Which phase helps the owner obtain and evaluate contractor offers?|Bidding or negotiation|Pre-feasibility|Post-construction management|Architect selection|The architect's bidding service assists procurement and contract award.
3|9|Design phases|What level of site presence is included in regular design construction services?|Periodic visits, not continuous full-time supervision|Continuous daily inspection by default|No site review of any kind|Contractor management as the builder|Regular construction-phase service includes periodic observation; full-time supervision is a separate service.
3|9|Design phases|Which service should be separately engaged for continuous site supervision?|SPP 204A full-time supervision|SPP 205 building administration|SPP 208 competition|SPP 201 pre-design|SPP 204A covers full-time supervision beyond regular periodic visits.
