3|12|Supervision|Does the construction supervision group replace the contractor's superintendent?|No, the contractor still runs the work|Yes, automatically|Only on government jobs|Only when the architect is absent|The CSG monitors and reports; it does not take over the contractor's execution duties.
3|12|Supervision|To whom does a supervision group report administratively?|The owner|The material supplier|The exam board|The competition jury|The module distinguishes administrative reporting to the owner from technical reporting to the AoR.
3|12|Supervision|To whom does a supervision group report technically?|The architect of record|The local tax assessor|The architect-selection jury|The tenant association|Technical coordination is with the AoR.
3|12|Management|Which SPP document covers construction management?|SPP 204B|SPP 204A|SPP 201|SPP 208|SPP 204B defines construction management services.
3|12|Management|Which three project outcomes does construction management coordinate closely?|Cost, time, and quality|Only shape, color, and texture|Only rent, tax, and insurance|Only publicity, sales, and branding|The construction manager coordinates the delivery program, cost, and quality.
3|13|Management|Can the construction manager assume the contractor's direct construction duties by default?|No, the contractor retains responsibility for building the work|Yes, all contractor duties transfer automatically|Yes, if the CM is an architect|Only when the owner asks verbally|The module separates construction management from the contractor's execution liability.
